ex8: Football Practice

Built on Willem A. (Vlakkies) Schreuder's CSCI4229/5229 ex8 starter.

Build and run (from this directory):
  make
  ./ex8

Requires a C compiler, OpenGL, GLU, and GLUT/freeglut development libraries.
The supplied makefile supports Linux, macOS, and MinGW.

Controls:
  Arrow keys / left mouse drag   Orbit the camera around the field
  + / - or mouse wheel          Zoom in / out
  Space                         Pause / resume animation
  R                             Replay from the beginning (retains pause state)
  0                             Restore the initial camera
  A                             Toggle world axes
  Esc                           Exit

The ten-second animation loops automatically: the blue player walks toward
an association football (soccer ball), draws back the right leg, kicks,
and follows through. The ball spins, follows diminishing ballistic bounces,
and slows down. Animation uses elapsed seconds rather than frame counts.
This is a choreographed animation, not a collision/rigid-body simulation.

Geometry and assignment requirements:
- Cube torso/hips, capped cylinder limbs, sphere head/joints and football.
- All vertices are generated in ex8.c; no canned GLUT/GLU solids or models.
- A generic articulated human is instantiated twice using translation,
  rotation and scaling: the full-size blue kicker and a smaller orange
  observer at a different location and orientation.
- Custom closed cleats are lofted from six elliptical cross sections, with
  varying width, height and center height, forming heel, instep and toe.
  They supply an original non-primitive mesh beyond the class example solids.
- Nested matrix transforms animate hips, knees, shoulders and elbows.
- A perspective orbit camera provides multiple eye positions.
- GL_DEPTH_TEST and a cleared depth buffer hide obstructed surfaces.
- Surface normals and primitive/cleat-side UVs support later lighting and
  texture work. No textures or external assets are required now.

The original example's matrix-stack approach, sphere parameterization,
text/error helpers, and display/reshape/keyboard callback structure remain
as the foundation; its object-selection demo has been replaced by this scene.

The assignment explicitly discourages simple primitive-only figures. This
scene adds a custom cleat mesh and articulation, but instructor acceptance
of the overall human concept should be checked if that restriction is strict.
